# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — environment contract. Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in.
# Values ship EMPTY by design; the comments describe the local-dev defaults that
# `pnpm db:up` (the Supabase local stack) provisions. No real credential ever
# belongs in this file — and note that the harness's own hygiene gate rejects
# JWT-shaped strings anywhere in the template, so even the well-known local anon
# key is written as a description here rather than pasted.

# ══ THE THREE CLASSES ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# This file is split by BLAST RADIUS, not by feature, because the three classes
# fail differently:
#
#   (a) server-only      — never leaves the server. A leak is a breach.
#   (b) NEXT_PUBLIC_*    — inlined into the WEB bundle at build time.
#   (c) EXPO_PUBLIC_*    — inlined into the NATIVE BINARY at build time.
#
# For (b) and (c) "inlined" means literally substituted into a shipped artifact.
# A secret behind a public prefix is not "exposed if someone looks" — it is
# PUBLISHED, and for (c) it is published inside a binary already on users'
# devices that you cannot recall. Rotating is the only remedy. @app/env parses
# the three classes with three separate schemas so a server-only key is not even
# reachable from a client bundle.

# ---- (a) server-only ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The service-role key BYPASSES row-level security — it is the one credential
# that can read every tenant's rows. Its only sanctioned home is an ADR-governed
# Edge Function (see supabase/functions/README.md). It must never be imported by
# apps/web or apps/mobile; the write-guard and the architecture gate both deny it.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Direct Postgres connection for migrations and the RLS isolation suite.
# Local-dev default (the Supabase stack's own): 127.0.0.1 port 54322, database
# postgres, user postgres, password postgres.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# ---- (b) web, public ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All three are public by construction: they appear in every request the browser
# makes, and — per the blast-radius framing above — `next build` INLINES them into
# the shipped JS bundle. So they are PUBLISHED, not merely "exposed if someone
# looks": treat them as world-readable, and let row-level security — NOT obscurity —
# be the only thing between a caller and another tenant's rows. A server-only key
# behind a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is a breach the moment it ships; the
# `next-public-secret-name` write-guard rule denies the KEY/SECRET/TOKEN shapes for
# exactly this reason.
# Blast radius vs (c): a leaked web value is RECALLABLE — rotate it, redeploy, and the
# next page load carries the new bundle. The native binary in (c) is already on users'
# devices and cannot be recalled; that asymmetry is why (c) is the graver of the two
# even though the mechanism — "inlined into a shipped artifact" — is identical.
# "PUBLISHABLE" is the current name for what older docs and `supabase status` still
# print as the "anon key" — @app/env's schema is the source of truth for these names,
# and it uses the new one.
# Local-dev default: the API URL is 127.0.0.1 port 54321; the key is printed by
# `supabase start` (and by `supabase status`).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE=
NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN=

# ---- (c) mobile, public ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The same values, under the prefix Expo inlines. They are duplicated rather than
# shared because the two bundlers read different prefixes — one variable cannot
# serve both, and aliasing them invites a server-only key into whichever prefix is
# read second.
#
# URL and WEB_ORIGIN are OPTIONAL here and only here: app.config.ts `extra` is a
# second, committed source for both, baked into the build. The publishable key has
# no second source, so its absence is a loud configuration error.
# Default web origin: {{WEB_ORIGIN}} (the committed app.config.ts extra).
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE=
EXPO_PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN=

# ---- (d) rate limiting, server-only, OPTIONAL ──────────────────────────────────
# The only optional pair in this file, and the only one whose ABSENCE is a supported
# configuration: with neither set, the limiter falls back to an IN-PROCESS sliding
# window and says so at error level. That fallback is a real limiter for one process
# and nothing more — on a platform running N instances it permits `limit × N`, and on
# a serverless one that discards the process between requests it permits everything.
#
# They are read in apps/web/lib/rate-limit-runtime.ts rather than in @app/env for
# exactly that reason: @app/env's server schema is fail-fast and its stated rule is
# that every entry there is a value whose disclosure is an incident. An optional pair
# in that list would make the rule untrue.
#
# The token is a credential. It grants full read/write on the Redis database, so it
# never rides a NEXT_PUBLIC_/EXPO_PUBLIC_ name and never reaches a client bundle.
# SOURCE: docs/adr/20260204-rate-limiting.md
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
